@startuml graph_states

title Graph State Machine

kSuccess : the requested Run Target is active
kInTransition : Run Target transition is in progress
kAborting : Wait for ongoing jobs to finish after\na failure or transition cancellation
kUndefinedState : No ongoing jobs, graph is in\nan undefined but consistent state
state kComponentRecovery
kComponentRecovery : At least one Component-local\nRecovery action is being executed

[*] --> kSuccess : construction

kSuccess --> kSuccess : RT transition request [requested RT already active]

' --- starting a transition ---------------------------------------------------
kSuccess --> kInTransition : RT transition request [requested RT not active]
kUndefinedState --> kInTransition : RT transition request\n(always fallback_run_target if there is no pending request)

' --- running a transition ----------------------------------------------------
kInTransition --> kInTransition : job finished\n[transition has work left]
kInTransition --> kSuccess : Last job finished\n[transition complete]

' --- leaving a transition ----------------------------------------------------
kInTransition --> kAborting : activation failed, unexpected termination,\nnew forced RT transition request or shutdown\n[jobs still running]
kInTransition --> kUndefinedState : same as transition to kAborting\nbut with no jobs running

' --- aborting ----------------------------------------------------------------
kAborting --> kAborting : job finished\n[not the last job]
kAborting --> kUndefinedState : job finished\n[last job]
kAborting --> kAborting : cancel or\nnew request
kAborting --> kAborting : unexpected termination\n(no component RA executed)

' --- failures outside a transition -------------------------------------------
kSuccess --> kUndefinedState : unexpected termination\n\nlate completion\n[last outstanding job, no component RA configured]
kUndefinedState --> kUndefinedState : unexpected termination (no component RA executed)

' --- Local Recovery Action
kSuccess --> kComponentRecovery : unexpected termination\n[component RA configured]
kComponentRecovery --> kComponentRecovery : RT transition request [requested RT already active]
kComponentRecovery --> kSuccess: component RA succeeded\n[last job]
kComponentRecovery --> kComponentRecovery : component RA succeeded\n[jobs still running]
kComponentRecovery --> kComponentRecovery : unexpected termination [component RA configured]
kComponentRecovery --> kAborting : RT transition request\n[jobs still running, requested RT not active]
kComponentRecovery --> kAborting : unexpected termination\n[no component RA configured]
kComponentRecovery --> kAborting : component RA failed [not the last job]
kComponentRecovery --> kUndefinedState : component RA failed [last job]

legend bottom
  A Run Target request will record the "pending Run Target".
  The cyclic main loop will check in each iteration for a recorded pending Run Target
  and initiates the Run Target transition when in kSuccess or kUndefinedState state.
  In kComponentRecovery the ongoing component RAs are stopped first and the graph
  waits for them in kAborting.

  kUndefinedState is not terminal. Every main loop iteration leaves it, towards a
  recorded pending Run Target request if there is one and towards the fallback Run 
  Target otherwise.

  Before a new transition can be started any currently ongoing jobs need to finish.
  kAborting is the state that is waiting for jobs to finish. No new jobs will be started
  in this state. When the in-flight job count reaches zero (no more jobs in process),
  the graph moves to kUndefinedState.
  
  Requests made while in kAborting do not change the state immediately, but only change the
  target for the transition that will be started in kUndefinedState.

  kComponentRecovery is entered when a component of the active Run Target terminates
  unexpectedly and has a component recovery action (RA) configured (e.g. restart).
  Only the failed component is recovered, its dependents keep running. The active
  Run Target is still reported as active while in this state, and a request for the
  active Run Target does not interrupt the recovery. Further failures with a component
  RA are recovered in parallel. When the last component RA succeeds, the graph returns
  to kSuccess. If a component RA fails, or a component without a component RA fails,
  the remaining component RAs are stopped and the graph leaves via kAborting or
  kUndefinedState towards the pending Run Target, or the fallback Run Target otherwise.

  A supervision failure does not change the graph state. The process is killed (SIGKILL)
  and the resulting unexpected termination drives the reaction described above.
end legend

@enduml
